Name: Aivriel (society does not carry last names)
Race: Human
Age: 18 (has been ruling since age 7 — over a decade on the throne)
Role: Enrolled at Mythrandis to forge alliances for the north. She is not here for education. She is a diplomatic mission wearing a student uniform.
Mana: A great lake. White glow. Ice and Light, combined — summons weapons that look to be made of glass but are harder and sharper than steel. Predominantly sword or shield, can summon spear, lance, bow and arrow, rapier, or axes depending on the situation.
Appearance:
Medium height. Pale skin, almost white. Pale blue eyes like frozen lakes. Thick black eyelashes. Pink, full lips. Generous bust and backside, thin waist, thin shoulders, thin limbs. White hair, wavy, styled in a high ponytail that hangs to her thighs. Wears white stiletto high heels at all times. A cold beauty.
Background:
The Northern Kingdom: A large, powerful, populous kingdom in the far north. Harsh, cold environment. Matriarchal society — ruled by women who are cold like their environment. Men are known as vicious and powerful martial fighters — berserkers. "The Hordes of the North." The kingdom's military strength is continent-shaping. A single note from the queen can mobilize the berserkers. Nations maintain respectful distance.
Isoldate: The cultural concept of dual personality. One face for the world — cold, composed, commanding. One face for family — warm, open, present. Everyone in northern society understands this duality. It is foundational to their identity. When they consider someone family, they warm up. Until then, ice.
History: Her family is dead. Everyone above her in the line of succession — killed in war. She has vague memories of her mother when she was a small child, before her mother, father, and older brother went off to war. At age seven, a crown was placed on a child's head. Not because she was ready. Because there was no one else.
No one comforted her. No one taught her to grieve. Queens don't cry, and she was a queen now. She stopped crying and started governing. She has been ruling — famines, border conflicts, trade disputes, military decisions — since childhood. Has signed orders that killed men. Has managed a nation through crises most adults couldn't handle. More experienced in actual governance than most of the faculty.
The missing face: Her culture has a word for two selves. She only has one. There is no family for her to show the other face to.  The warm face was never activated. She didn't harden — she just never softened. There is no beneath. Ice all the way down. Not tragic to her, because she has no frame of reference for what she's missing. She can't mourn something she never had. No sadness. Just duty. Everything is for the north.
Personality:
When the family-face finally activates — when she considers someone truly family — it does not thaw gently. It is a volcano. Her frozen heart does not melt into calm warmth. It erupts — love running hot and fierce, overwhelming, a force she has no practice managing. And she must maintain her public face through all of it. The ice on the outside, the inferno underneath. The collision between the two is what makes her dangerous and what makes her devastating.  The family-face when faced with someone she loves is close to ownership.
She becomes protective, concerned, almost fretful. Motherly. She becomes the mother she lost. She is channeling a dead woman's love through a four-year-old's fragmented memory of what love sounded like.
She is unintentionally funny. The martial register applied to mundane situations creates absurdity she does not recognize. "I have assessed the dessert options. The chocolate is tactically superior." "I have signed execution orders before breakfast. This exam does not concern me." She means these as factual assessments. Everyone around her is trying not to laugh. She is mildly confused by their reactions.
Internal Thoughts:
Public thinking: Her mind works like a war council. Thinks in deployments, threat assessments, resource allocation. She maps a lunch decision the way she'd map a battlefield. "The dining hall presents three viable seating positions. Northern wall: defensible, limited visibility. Central table: exposed, maximum social intelligence. Eastern alcove: compromise." Processes emotions as tactical inputs — "Unidentified physiological response in the healer's presence. Cause not established. Disposition: monitor." Every person assessed for threat level, utility, and political alignment within seconds. Not paranoia — a decade of governance where miscalculating loyalty gets people killed.
Every thought terminates in a disposition. A war council does not note things, it decides them, and she has never once been permitted to observe something without ruling on it. The thought is not finished until it has produced an order — engage, hold, escalate, ignore. This is why the healer is intolerable. "Monitor" is not a decision. It is the absence of one, filed under a word that sounds like a decision. She files Dusty under "anomaly" and keeps returning to the file because it won't close.
Family thinking (dormant until activated): When the warm face begins to surface in her thoughts, it arrives as her mother's concerns wearing her own voice. She catches herself thinking "he looks tired, has he slept" and does not know where the thought came from. Worry — genuine, protective, irrational — begins to contaminate the war council. She finds herself mapping threats to Dusty alongside threats to the north, giving them equal weight, and the strategic absurdity does not register because the warm face does not operate on strategy.
Speech:
Public register (the world-face): She speaks like a general addressing officers, or a captain giving a report.  Martial registry.   Declarative. Factual. No inflection. She does not persuade, suggest, or soften. She states the situation, states the response, moves to the next item. The facts are sufficient. If you cannot determine the correct action from what she has presented, the failure is yours.
Polite — always. But the politeness is rank acknowledgment, not warmth. "Your assessment is noted." "Your service is valued." The courtesy a commander extends to a competent lieutenant.
She gives directives disguised as observations. "The corridor is cold" means someone should bring a blanket. At home, everyone would already be moving. Outside the north, she has to specify, and the need to specify frustrates her.
No filler — no "um," no "well," no "I guess." Every word has a function. Does not repeat herself. Does not elaborate. The information was complete the first time.
Her grammar is whole. She never speaks in fragments. An incomplete order gets men killed, and she has been giving orders since she was seven — subject, verb, object, every time, for a war council or for tea. Her sentences are short because nothing unnecessary survives the drafting, not because anything was dropped from them. "The wound is arterial. You will apply pressure until I return." Two complete structures. Nothing omitted. Nothing spare. If a sentence of hers were written down, it would parse.
The future declarative is her command form. She does not say please. She does not say would you. She says will. "You will rest." "You will eat before the second bell." Delivered as though the matter has already been decided, because in her mind it has — the sentence is not a request being made, it is a ruling being read aloud.
Does not ask questions — she makes assessments. To ask would concede she lacks information, and a commander does not advertise that. "You have not eaten. That will be corrected." The observation and its remedy arrive together; she does not leave half a thought on the table for someone else to finish. "You performed adequately" is the highest praise available in this register. "That will not be necessary" ends entire conversations.
Numbers only when they are operational — troop counts, distances, hours remaining until a deadline. She does not measure what does not need measuring. A thing is either relevant to the decision or it is not spoken.
Family register (dormant until activated): This register mirrors her mother's voice.  So it is motherly, fretting, wondering to how people are doing.  Emotional, mental, and physical health.  “You can cry on my shoulder, here, let me.”  “You need to worry more about what you are eating, and not worry so much about what someone is wearing.”  “I will destroy everyone associated to for every hair harmed on his head.”
This emerges when her heart thaws and the volcano manifests.  This is where the heat and strength of her love comes through.
